# coverage.thresholds — per-package minimum statement coverage (percent).
#
# Format:  <import-path> <min-pct>     (per-package override)
#          default <min-pct>            (floor for any gated package not listed)
#          # comment / blank lines ignored
#
# Gate lives in scripts/coverage.sh (`make cover`). Packages matching the
# exclusion regex there (cmd/*, telemetry, connectorpb, pgtest/oidctest/
# sdktest, e2e) are measured but never gated — no entry needed.
#
# RATCHET: floors only ever rise. After raising coverage, run `make cover-bump`
# to rewrite this file to achieved-minus-epsilon, then commit.
#
# NOTE: seeded during M7 Workstream A with a permissive default so the gate can
# land green; Workstream B raises the weak packages, then `make cover-bump`
# tightens these floors. See docs/adr/0022-testing-and-benchmark-policy.md.




# TC-016 (docs/assurance/test-conformance.md): raised 0 -> 80 on 2026-08-09.
#
# `default` applies to any gated package with no line of its own — which today
# is none, because every existing package is listed. It therefore governs
# exactly one thing: what happens when a NEW package lands. At 0 it landed
# ungated and silently stayed that way; the ratchet only ever protected
# packages someone had already thought about.
#
# 80 is the stated floor for this codebase, and the seeding comment above
# always intended the permissive default to be temporary. A new package that
# genuinely cannot reach it (live-I/O connectors are the recurring case) is
# still free to carry a lower floor — but as an explicit line with a reason,
# the way azureblobconn and edi do below, rather than by default.
default 80

github.com/aaron-au/shift/connectors/internal/fsconn 81
github.com/aaron-au/shift/connectors/internal/soapconn 85
github.com/aaron-au/shift/connectors/internal/smtpconn 76
